prompt:
Spawn, objective 212 [feature] (GitHub #63 part 2, filed 2026-11-16 on mission 2; the lead's word of 2026-11-17 16:1x; design narrowed 16:50; author rev-j): the supervisor's exact duplicate brief in flight is refused at assignment, naming the task it repeats.

Base: live main 0905330d.

The problem (#63, mission 2): tasks 13 and 14 got the same judge-audit brief; 14 was cancelled and 17 was made with that brief again, and two crawls ran side by side in different workspaces against the same host. Nothing compared a new brief with the work in flight, and the record of task 17 did not say what it repeated.

Read first:
- crates/spawn-postgres/sql/schema.sql: spawn_task_assign, spawn_mission_caller (owner, spawn_supervisor or null), and the two grant blocks (the mission functions closed to public; the supervisor's list);
- crates/spawn-postgres/src/missions.rs (task_assign);
- crates/spawn-cli/src/missions.rs (`spawn task assign`, Flags::parse_with_switches as `mission configure` uses it);
- crates/spawn-cli/src/serve.rs task_in_flight (what the board counts as in flight).

What changes:
(1) spawn_task_assign takes p_again boolean default false (the old 5-argument function is dropped, as the 4-argument one already is; both grant lists name the new signature). Under the append lock, after its existing checks, it looks for a task of the same mission whose brief equals the new brief with leading and trailing space, tabs, CR and LF trimmed on both (btrim with E' \t\r\n') and that is in flight: state assigned, delivered, reported, revise or landing, or accepted in a mission with a gate (it still waits for its gate); the lowest such id.
(2) When there is one, the caller is spawn_supervisor and p_again is not true, it raises "task <id> of mission <m> has the same brief and is <state>: a duplicate is not assigned (cancel it first, or assign again on purpose with --again)". Nothing is recorded and no task is made.
(3) From the owner (a person or a script: one who gives several workers one brief means it; about 20 Rust tests and 15 scenarios do so on purpose), or with p_again, the task is assigned. Whenever there was such a task, the task.assigned record carries duplicate_of: its id. A task with no duplicate in flight records no duplicate_of.
(4) PostgresStore::task_assign keeps its signature (again false); task_assign_again(..., again) passes it. `spawn task assign ... [--again]`: --again is a switch that takes no value; the usage text says what it does.

What stays as it is: spawn_objective_assign (it already allows one task per objective in flight); briefs that only look alike (exact match after the trim, as #63 suggests); the same brief in another mission; every other caller check and record field.

Files: crates/spawn-postgres/sql/schema.sql (spawn_task_assign, the two grant lists), crates/spawn-postgres/src/missions.rs (task_assign, task_assign_again), crates/spawn-cli/src/missions.rs (split_task, assign's --again, USAGE), crates/spawn-postgres/tests/duplicates.rs (new); owner: rsi/scenarios/212.sh, rsi/backlog/212.json.

Acceptance: rsi/scenarios/212.sh, black box on a fresh scenario database, through `spawn task assign` with the database's own supervisor login (a password made up per run, handed over in a 0600 passfile, never on a command line) and with the owner's, `spawn task cancel`, `spawn task accept` and `spawn mission list`:
- (1) from the supervisor, the brief of a task in flight in the same mission, as is and with other leading and trailing space, is refused: exit not 0, the error naming that task, the mission and its state ("task <id> of mission <m> has the same brief and is assigned"); no task is made;
- (2) the same brief in another mission, and another brief in the same mission, are assigned;
- (3) --again assigns it; mission list shows both tasks;
- (4) with every task of the brief cancelled, it is assigned;
- (5) the owner's duplicate is assigned, and its task.assigned record names the task it repeats; so does the record of (3)'s --again;
- (6) a task accepted in a mission with no gate is done: its brief is assigned again; in a gated mission a task delivered, reported, sent back (revise) or accepted is in flight: the supervisor's same brief is refused, naming the state;
- (7) that task, once its gate accepts it (task.gated: landing), is still in flight, refused naming landing; landed (task.landed), it is done and the brief is assigned.
On live main 0905330d, 212.sh exits 1.

Tests (crates/spawn-postgres/tests/duplicates.rs, through the supervisor's login and the owner's): the refusal's words and that nothing is recorded; the owner's duplicate assigned with duplicate_of; another brief or another mission with no duplicate_of; delivered, reported and revise in flight, their state named; again with duplicate_of; cancelled not in flight; accepted in an ungated mission not in flight, in a gated one in flight; landing in flight; landed not. No test is renamed or dropped.

Not in 212: the delivered-task activity (211, landed), the paper cuts (213), and merging or comparing briefs that only look alike.

Verified by: the gate runs 212.sh. Live, the next time the supervisor assigns a brief a task of its mission in flight already has, its pane gets the refusal naming that task, and nothing is assigned.
